# 10. Comparison of All Approaches

| Approach       | Time        | Space | Sorted Output   | Interview Rating |
| -------------- | ----------- | ----- | --------------- | ---------------- |
| Brute Force    | O(high−low) | O(1)  | Yes             | ⭐                |
| Simulation     | O(1)        | O(1)  | Yes             | ⭐⭐⭐⭐             |
| BFS            | O(1)        | O(1)  | Yes             | ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐            |
| DFS            | O(1)        | O(1)  | No (needs sort) | ⭐⭐⭐⭐             |
| Sliding Window | O(1)        | O(1)  | Yes             | ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐            |

---

# 11. Interview Notes

* There are only **36 valid sequential-digit numbers**, so generating candidates is much better than checking every number.
* **BFS** is often the preferred interview solution because it generates numbers in ascending order without sorting.
* **Sliding Window** is the shortest and cleanest solution, using the fact that every valid number is a substring of `"123456789"`.
* **DFS** is easy to explain recursively but requires sorting because it does not naturally produce ascending order.
* If an interviewer asks for optimization after a brute-force solution, highlight the **limited search space** and switch to a generation-based approach.

---

# 12. Key Takeaways

* Avoid brute force over the entire range.
* Recognize patterns that drastically reduce the search space.
* BFS and Sliding Window are the most interview-friendly approaches.
* Simulation demonstrates constructive number generation.
* DFS is a good recursive alternative but requires sorting.
* Remember the key fact: **only 36 sequential-digit numbers exist**, which is why optimized solutions run in constant time.
